would be 2.8. Not lab high, but higher scale. Or is the hemoglobin A1C close to normal

00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:34,280
seems a bit high, but not sure. I have followed this test before. So you can change your blood

00:00:34,280 --> 00:00:41,800
glucose levels quite quickly compared to you being able to change your hemoglobin A1C levels.

00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:51,640
That's why really a better look at a person's blood picture for diabetes is hemoglobin A1C.

00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:56,920
Because you could get, you know, false low glucose levels, because

00:00:57,560 --> 00:01:06,360
person with diabetes or hypoglycemia issues, or in that range where your prediabetic

00:01:07,240 --> 00:01:12,760
can have a spike of glucose after the, and then it actually plummet down. So it's actually

00:01:12,760 --> 00:01:17,800
lower than normal. And if they're not taking either blood glucose at a regular time each time

00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:24,360
with a 12 hour fast, they could get some kind of false good glucose levels. We're actually

00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:30,920
their glucose. On a 12 hour fast is raging in the hundreds, but they're getting false

00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:35,560
glucose levels. Now that's not what this person is asking, but I just made that point because

00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:41,240
hemoglobin A1C won't bounce up and down through the day. Hemoglobin A1C is a much better

00:01:43,640 --> 00:01:51,560
lab value to look at to monitor diabetes than fasting glucose is. The reason why I want you to look

00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:58,360
at fasting glucose is because it's easy to take, easy to do, easy to monitor at home, and easy to

00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:04,840
monitor, easy to prevent those problems that I just mentioned, because you're going to take it every

00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:09,640
morning right when you wake up. You have a 12 hour fast already. So you're going to have some

00:02:09,640 --> 00:02:17,560
consistency of when you're taking it. You won't move your hemoglobin A1C or your insulin as fast.

00:02:17,560 --> 00:02:24,360
So as you've changed your diet and you're keeping your fasting glucose at below 80 on a fairly

00:02:24,360 --> 00:02:33,320
regular basis, the hemoglobin A1C and the insulin levels will follow. Understand the insulin levels are

00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:40,360
also a function of your pancreas and your cell membranes as are your hemoglobin A1C. It takes time

00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:50,360
to heal those. So you just have to be patient with your cell.

